# Live Entry Radar RTH evaluator — the product lane
# (research/live_entry_radar/W4_LIVE_EVALUATOR_DESIGN.md §2, §3). Same reason the
# Prophet evaluator lives on the box rather than in Actions: GitHub throttles this
# repo's frequent crons far past the documented 15-45 min (measured 2026-07-30: a
# */5 lane ran 09:07Z then 12:19Z, ~3h12m apart), so a 5-minute product cadence is
# not purchasable there at any price. .github/workflows/entry-radar-live.yml is the
# dormant backstop, not the cadence.
#
# The pass reads the box's own quote plane off disk (the SAME artifacts the Prophet
# lane reads — this lane fetches nothing itself), evaluates the frozen nightly pack
# through the unmodified W3 oracle, spools transitions to R2 and writes the payload
# into the MACRO_LIVE_DIR ladder by atomic rename. It writes no data/ path and runs
# no git command (LEDGER LAW G0.2; design §0 "no W5 writer").
#
# RESOURCE CAPS ARE INHERITED FROM THE PROPHET LANE'S MEASURED ENVELOPE, NOT
# MEASURED HERE. This unit ships DORMANT (update.sh installs it only behind
# ENTRY_RADAR_LIVE_ENABLE=1; design §3b), so there has been no armed production pass
# to measure — and inventing numbers for one would be worse than inheriting numbers
# somebody did measure. macro-live-prophet.service is the right reference because it
# is the same SHAPE of job on the same box: a 5-minute oneshot, in the same ET
# window, consuming the same quote artifacts, publishing to the same two planes
# (R2 + the live dir), and its header records the measurement (~95 MB peak RSS,
# wall clock dominated by 2-4 R2 round trips, on a 2-vCPU box with thin burst
# headroom).
#
# Where this lane HONESTLY differs, and why the inherited numbers still hold:
#   * it runs the real indicator chain over the probe set instead of comparing a
#     price to a pre-solved level, so its per-pass compute is larger than Prophet's
#     measured 0.006 s — how much larger is unknown until an armed session says so;
#   * MemoryHigh 256M is a SOFT throttle (reclaim pressure, not a kill), so a first
#     armed pass that wants more slows down and is visible in the health receipt
#     rather than dying; MemoryMax 512M is the hard stop that bounds a runaway;
#   * CPUQuota 60% cannot starve the quote lanes this pass depends on (fast 180%,
#     snapshot 90%), and Nice/CPUWeight/IOWeight sit in the `bars` tier — the LOWEST
#     on the box, deliberately: this lane CONSUMES what the quote lanes publish, so
#     it must always lose a scheduling contest with them.
# TimeoutStartSec 120 bounds a wedged R2 call or vendor read well inside the 300 s
# timer period, so two passes can never overlap (the state-dir flock is the second
# guard, not the first). The §15 acceptance line — "5-min cadence measured across a
# full RTH session" — is what replaces this inheritance with a measurement, and it
# completes at operator activation (research/live_entry_radar/W4_DEPLOY_PLAN.md §3).
[Unit]
Description=Mastermind Live Entry Radar intraday RTH evaluator
After=network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=oneshot
WorkingDirectory=/opt/macro
EnvironmentFile=-/etc/macro-live.env
ExecStart=/opt/macro/.venv/bin/python -m scripts.entry_radar_live
TimeoutStartSec=120
Nice=10
CPUWeight=20
CPUQuota=60%
MemoryHigh=256M
MemoryMax=512M
IOWeight=20
NoNewPrivileges=true
PrivateTmp=true

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
